[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Performance Monitoring in PHP with New Relic"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Performance monitoring is crucial for maintaining the health and efficiency of your PHP applications. Tools like New Relic provide valuable insights into application performance, allowing you to identify and resolve bottlenecks, optimize code, and ensure a smooth user experience. This tutorial will guide you through setting up and using New Relic for performance monitoring in your PHP environment."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Prerequisites"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"A PHP application (e.g., built with Laravel, Symfony, or a custom framework)."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"A New Relic account (sign up at newrelic.com)."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Access to your server's command line."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Installation and Configuration"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"The first step is to install the New Relic PHP agent on your server. The installation process varies depending on your operating system and PHP environment. Here's a general guide:"},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"# Debian/Ubuntu:
sudo apt-get update
sudo apt-get install newrelic-php5

# CentOS/RHEL:
sudo yum install newrelic-php5"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"After installation, you'll need to configure the New Relic agent. The configuration file is typically located at `/etc/newrelic/newrelic.ini`. Open this file with a text editor."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"sudo nano /etc/newrelic/newrelic.ini"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Find the `newrelic.license` setting and replace `YOUR_LICENSE_KEY` with your New Relic license key. You can find your license key in your New Relic account settings."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"newrelic.license = "YOUR_LICENSE_KEY""},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Also, set the `newrelic.appname` setting to the name of your application. This will help you identify your application in the New Relic dashboard."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"newrelic.appname = "My PHP Application""},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"Save the changes to `newrelic.ini` and restart your web server (e.g., Apache or Nginx) and PHP-FPM to apply the new configuration."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"# Restart Apache:
sudo systemctl restart apache2

# Restart PHP-FPM:
sudo systemctl restart php7.4-fpm  # Adjust version as needed"},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Using the New Relic Dashboard"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"Once the agent is installed and configured, and your application is running, data will start flowing to the New Relic dashboard. Log in to your New Relic account and navigate to the APM (Application Performance Monitoring) section. Select your application from the list."},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"The dashboard provides a wealth of information about your application's performance, including:"},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Response time: The average time it takes for your application to respond to requests."},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Throughput: The number of requests your application handles per minute."},{"order":23,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Error rate: The percentage of requests that result in errors."},{"order":24,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Transaction traces: Detailed information about individual requests, including database queries, external services calls, and code execution time."},{"order":25,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Database performance: Insights into the performance of your database queries, including slow queries and database connection issues."},{"order":26,"type":"subheading","text":"Custom Instrumentation"},{"order":27,"type":"paragraph","text":"New Relic allows you to add custom instrumentation to track specific parts of your code. This is useful for monitoring the performance of critical functions or business transactions. You can use the `newrelic_start_transaction()` and `newrelic_end_transaction()` functions to define custom transactions."},{"order":28,"type":"code","text":"<?php
newrelic_start_transaction('MyCustomTransaction');

// Your code here

newrelic_end_transaction();
?>"},{"order":29,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can also use `newrelic_name_transaction()` to give a more descriptive name to a transaction."},{"order":30,"type":"code","text":"<?php
newrelic_name_transaction('CheckoutProcess');
?>"},{"order":31,"type":"paragraph","text":"To track specific functions, you can use `newrelic_start_segment()` and `newrelic_end_segment()`."},{"order":32,"type":"code","text":"<?php
newrelic_start_segment('MyCustomSegment');

// Your code here

newrelic_end_segment('MyCustomSegment');
?>"},{"order":33,"type":"subheading","text":"Troubleshooting"},{"order":34,"type":"paragraph","text":"If you're not seeing data in the New Relic dashboard, check the following:"},{"order":35,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Verify that the New Relic agent is installed and running correctly. Check the agent's log file for any errors (usually located at `/var/log/newrelic/newrelic-daemon.log`)."},{"order":36,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Ensure that the license key is correct in the `newrelic.ini` file."},{"order":37,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Make sure that your web server and PHP-FPM have been restarted after installing and configuring the agent."},{"order":38,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Check your PHP error logs for any errors related to the New Relic agent."},{"order":39,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":40,"type":"paragraph","text":"New Relic is a powerful tool for performance monitoring in PHP applications. By installing and configuring the New Relic agent, you can gain valuable insights into your application's performance, identify bottlenecks, and optimize your code for a better user experience. Remember to leverage custom instrumentation to monitor critical parts of your application and proactively address any performance issues."}]
